# Let a viewer's key frame request reach the publisher, and make the
# unconditional key frame clock switchable.
#
# The problem, measured on a real line (2026-07-28):
#
# MediaMTX asks every WebRTC publisher for a key frame every two seconds,
# from a hardwired ticker (inbound_track.go), whether anybody needs one or
# not. For an encoder running intra refresh — where the refresh is spread
# across many frames instead of arriving as one big key frame — that clock
# puts the bursts straight back in: roughly one arrival gap per second at
# the viewer, against 0.2 with the clock effectively disabled.
#
# Turning the clock off alone is not a fix, it is a worse bug. A viewer
# that loses a packet can only re-sync at a key frame, and it does ask for
# one — but the request died in outbound_track.go, where incoming RTCP was
# unmarshalled and thrown away. Observed consequence: after nine lost
# packets the picture froze permanently while data kept arriving, until
# the peer connection gave up.
#
# So this patch builds the missing return path and only then makes the
# clock optional:
#
# 1. stream.Stream gains SetOnKeyFrameRequest/RequestKeyFrame. The Stream
#    is the one object publisher and readers already share, so it is the
#    natural bridge; Path is too thin (Name/SafeConf/Remove*) and there is
#    no other channel between the two sessions.
# 2. outbound_track.go inspects the RTCP it already reads and forwards
#    PictureLossIndication / FullIntraRequest to PeerConnection.OnKeyFrameRequest.
# 3. peer_connection.go carries that callback and gains RequestKeyFrame(),
#    which pokes its inbound video tracks.
# 4. inbound_track.go gains a throttled RequestKeyFrame() (at most one per
#    500 ms, so a hundred viewers on a lossy minute cost a handful of key
#    frames, not a hundred) and reads the ticker period from
#    PULSE_KEYFRAME_INTERVAL.
#    Unset keeps upstream's 2s behaviour, "0"/"off" disables the ticker.
# 5. servers/webrtc/session.go wires both ends: the publishing session
#    registers its connection as the path's key frame handler and clears it
#    on the way out; the reading session points its connection at the
#    stream's request method.
#
# Why an environment variable rather than a config key: it keeps the patch
# off conf schema, validation and docs, which are the parts most likely to
# conflict on the next upstream bump. An unconfigured deployment behaves
# exactly like upstream.
#
# When to drop this fork: as soon as upstream forwards receiver PLIs to
# publishers and lets the key frame interval be configured. Both are
# reasonable upstream features; this patch is the shape we would propose.
#
